# HTSA close pages — the short enrollment instrument

## Relationship to the older rule

`.cursor/rules/htsa-enrollment-invoice-workflow.mdc` covers the **legacy long
enrollment page** (`htsa-enrollment-*.html`, ~10,000px, locked pay zone).
**This rule takes priority for anything CJ asks for as a "close page".**
Do not merge the two templates. Do not port sections from the legacy page into
this one. Do not modify existing legacy pages when building a new close page.

## Trigger

CJ pastes a name, or a name plus email, phone, and options, in **this chat**.
He does **not** run Terminal. Never tell him to type `htsa-close`, open a
tab, or press Ctrl-D. You run the command. He copies the text and email
you print.

**Name only** (page already exists):

```bash
python3 scripts/htsa-send-pack.py --full-name "<Full Name>"
```

**New person or new pricing** (full block):

```bash
python3 scripts/htsa-paste-close.py --ship <<'EOF'
<paste his block>
EOF
```

Do this first. Do not read other client files. Do not ask follow-up questions.

Wait for `READY` plus a URL before you give him the enroll link. The 30 day
plan is already on that page at the bottom. Do not send a second roadmap URL
unless he asks for it.

After `READY` (or after send-pack), **always** print TEXT and EMAIL in chat
as a black-on-white HTML block he can paste into Gmail and HubSpot. One
enroll link. Clickable. No markdown. No code fences. Use the block in Step 8.

## What this page is for

CJ is **on the phone or Zoom with them right now**. They have already verbally
agreed to a plan. This page confirms a decision that was already made. It is not
a brochure and it is not for reading alone. Every extra word on it costs money.

Target: under 3,500px on a 390px viewport. If a change pushes it past that, cut something.

## Never hand-rebuild

```
SOURCE:  templates/_TEMPLATE-close.html
OUTPUT:  htsa-enrollment-<first>-<last>.html      (repo root, matches existing convention)
```

Live URL becomes `https://closewithcjclay.com/htsa-enrollment-<first>-<last>.html`

Never write one from scratch. Never "improve" the template's layout, CSS, copy,
guarantee wording, or tracking while making someone's page.

## Step 1 — copy, then edit ONLY the BUYER block

```js
const BUYER = {
  firstName: "Test",
  fullName:  "Test Person",
  email:     "test@example.com",
  phone:     "+15555550100",              // E.164
  slug:      "test_person",               // must match the filename
  track:     "closer",                    // "closer" or "setter"
  offer:     "standard",                  // "standard" or "reactivation"
  show:      ["pif", "plan", "clarity"]   // which options appear, in this order
};
```

`slug` drives the rows in CJ's Google Sheet. If it does not match the filename his
tracking breaks silently. Check it.

`show` is how CJ controls what is offered. If he says "PIF and ClarityPay only",
set `show: ["pif","clarity"]` and the 4-pay card is removed from the DOM entirely.

## Step 2 — the offer modes

Never edit prices inline. Set `offer` and the whole set swaps.

| | standard | reactivation |
|---|---|---|
| PIF | $6,000 | $5,000 |
| Plan | 4 × $1,750 = $7,000 | 3 × $1,750 = $5,250 |
| ClarityPay | $600/mo · $7,200 total | $500/mo · $6,000 total |

## Step 3 — a negotiated one-off price

If CJ says he agreed a custom number on the call, uncomment `CUSTOM_OFFER` and fill it.
It renders as a navy card **above** every other option and removes the "best value"
badge from PIF.

Never put a negotiated price in a paragraph without a working button. The legacy
page did that and the best offer became the only one the person could not buy.

